---
// Opening story for the Exemplars page: the reader decides first, then opens each outcome.
---
<section class="story-intro" aria-labelledby="story-heading">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">A fictional scenario</p>
    <h2 id="story-heading">A decision on Monday morning</h2>
    <p class="story-scene">Maple Campus has no business analyst. A club organizer complains that their room was double-booked the day before a 60-person workshop, the third complaint like it since September. Maya Chen, who manages student services, has seen this before, and a vendor’s online booking system could start next month.</p>

    <div class="story-decision">
      <h3>You are Maya. What do you decide?</h3>
      <ul class="decision-options">
        <li><strong>A</strong> Approve the new booking system now.</li>
        <li><strong>B</strong> Tell Facilities to answer every request within one working day.</li>
        <li><strong>C</strong> Find out why confirmations are slow, then choose a fix.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Choose before you read on. What would you need to know to be confident?</p>
    </div>

    <details class="faq-item story-reveal">
      <summary>What happened when Maya decided on instinct</summary>
      <div class="story-answer">
        <p>Maya approved the system. Six months later the budget is spent, but requests still arrive incomplete through email and chat, and one coordinator still approves every booking. Confirmations are a little faster; the complaints continue. Option B stalls in the same place, because replying faster does not fill in missing details or cover an absent approver.</p>
        <p>Neither choice was careless. Both drew on real experience, but nobody had checked what was causing the delay.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="faq-item story-reveal">
      <summary>The same decision, with a business analyst</summary>
      <div class="story-answer">
        <p>A business analyst, Jordan Patel, asks first: where do requests wait, what is missing when they arrive, and who approves when the coordinator is away? A <a href="#facilitation">workshop</a> and a sample of recent requests point to <a href="#causes">three likely causes</a>: incomplete requests from scattered channels, a single approver and a hand-updated calendar.</p>
        <p>Maya still decides, but now she approves a four-week pilot with <a href="#requirements">clear targets</a> and a plan for <a href="#risks">what could go wrong</a>. If it works, she has evidence to expand. If not, being wrong costs four weeks, not a system contract. The exemplars below show the work behind that decision.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
